Chapter: Linear

 Linearity is the discipline behind safe mutation. A value typed
 `linear T` is uniquely owned: exactly one binding holds it, and it
 must be used exactly once. While it is held it may be updated in
 place, because no one else can observe the change.

 `freeze` is the one-way door out of that discipline. It consumes a
 linearly owned value and hands back an ordinary immutable `T` that may
 be shared freely. Because the linear value is unique and is consumed
 here, no surviving reference can ever witness a later mutation -- so
 freeze needs no copy. It is the identity, and its whole meaning lives
 in the type: `linear a` goes in, a shareable `a` comes out, and the
 original binding is spent.

Section: Definitions

  freeze : linear a -> a
  freeze (x) = x

